Ministry of Environment Awards Cambodian Energy Efficiency Competition to Private Sector Operators
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Environment has awarded the winners of the Cambodian Energy Efficiency Competition to the winners from the private sector, the public sector and educational institutions under the chairmanship of Mr. Tin Ponlok, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment.
Mr. Tin Ponlok said that "Cambodia Energy Efficiency Competition" is the first program in Cambodia on energy efficiency competition for public and private buildings. The main purpose of this competition is to focus on different aspects of energy efficiency, ranging from consumers and changing daily practices and attitudes.
Tin Ponlok added that Cambodia is one of the most vulnerable countries to the effects of climate change. Cambodia's main priority is to build economic and social resilience to mitigate the effects of climate change on health, infrastructure and agricultural productivity.
In addition, Mr. Tin Ponlok emphasized that although Cambodia emits less greenhouse gases, the government has increased its commitment to specific strategies to contribute globally to climate change mitigation.
The winners, sponsors and organizers of the event explained why they are participating and committed to the Cambodian Energy Efficiency Competition.
On this occasion, SEVEA and the Institute of Technology of Cambodia (ITC), the organizers of the competition, also provided key points to better understand the importance of increasing awareness of energy efficiency and savings. Power, too.
The team also thanked the National Council for Sustainable Development and the Ministry of Environment for their support through the third phase of the Cambodian Climate Change Alliance, which received funding from the European Union, Sweden and UNDP. The team would also like to thank Schneider Electric.
