This Morning, Samdech Techo Hun Sen Opens a High-Level Forum on the 10th Anniversary of the ASEAN Agreement
Please note that the ASEAN Agreement is an initiative of ASEAN and was announced to start negotiations in 2012 in Phnom Penh when Cambodia chaired ASEAN. The talks were started by 16 countries, but India later withdrew.
The Asean agreement covers an estimated 2.2 billion people, or 30% of the world's population, with a GDP of 26.2 trillion US dollars or 30% of GDP. In total, the world trade volume is about 28% of the total.
The agreement provides about 90 percent regional tariff preferences. The agreement covers a wide range of key areas, including trade in goods, trade in services, investment permits, standards and techniques, sanitary and phytosanitary inspection, animal and plant sanitation, economic and technical cooperation, and intellectual property. Electronics, dispute resolution and other fields.
This agreement will make shopping cheaper, tourism easier, enterprises more confident and investment more efficient.
According to a study by the Economic Research Institute for ASEAN and East Asia (ERIA), Cambodia is expected to grow 7.3% in annual exports, 23.4% in investment and 2% of GDP. % On current GDP.
Cambodia enjoys preferential tariffs from this agreement on a number of key commodities. Cambodia's preferential tariffs include:
Cambodia will also benefit from the agreement, including the transfer of technology, knowledge, skills and new skills from foreign direct investment, as well as the creation of new jobs for the Cambodian people. The agreement will also boost growth in exports and gross domestic product, as well as help revive Cambodia's economy, which has been hit hard by Kovid 19.
The ASEAN Agreement aims to create an open trade and investment environment to facilitate regional trade and investment expansion and contribute to economic growth and global development. The ASEAN Agreement will be a core framework for trade and investment to further expand the regional value chain, bring market and employment opportunities for businesses and people in the region.
This agreement has 20 chapters with the following summary:
Chapter 1 Original Provisions and General Definitions: Define the objectives of the ASP Negotiations to establish a modern, high-quality and mutually beneficial economic partnership, taking into account development differences. And the economic needs of the parties, especially for less developed countries.
Chapter 4: Customs Procedures and Trade Facilitation: Create a conducive environment for the development of regional and global supply chains through simple, predictable and transparent regulations and the implementation of customs laws and regulations; Effective management of customs procedures and expedited release of goods in accordance with international best practices and standards.
Chapter 5 Sanitation and Hygienic Hygiene: sets out the basic framework for the preparation, adoption and implementation of sanitary and phytosanitary measures in order to ensure food safety and protect human, animal, plant or health life by ensuring hygienic and phytosanitary measures. Apply only when absolutely necessary to protect health and reduce the impact on trade.
Chapter 6 Technical Standards, Conformity and Compliance Assessment Procedures: Strengthen the implementation of the WTO Technical Barrier Agreement and enhance mutual understanding of the technical standards and procedures for conformity assessment. Each party also strengthens and cooperates in exchanging information with each other to reduce unnecessary technical barriers for trade.
Chapter 7 Trade Facilitation: Introduces protection and anti-dumping measures and tax measures that allow each party to use mechanisms to address losses or threaten losses to local industries based on rights. And the obligations of each party set out in the relevant agreements in the WTO.
Chapter 8: Trade in Services: Widened liberalization, which will pave the way for better trade in services through the reduction of restrictions and discriminatory measures for cross-border trade in services.
Chapter 9: Migration of Individuals: sets out the obligations of the parties to the ASEAN Agreement regarding the facilitation of entry and temporary residence of persons involved in trade in goods, supplies, services or investments, subject to conditions and conditions. Set in the contract table, such as the length of stay.
Chapter 11: Intellectual Property: Introduces a balanced and environmentally friendly approach to protecting and enforcing regional intellectual property laws, as well as enhancing the protection of intellectual property in addition to the framework provided under WTO agreements include: measures to protect technology and the exercise of digital rights, and electronic applications appropriate to the advent of the digital age.
Chapter 12: E-Commerce: Promote the use of e-commerce and cooperation among the parties. The Asean parties also agreed to maintain the current practice of non-implementation of tariffs for e-commerce in accordance with the decision of the WTO Minister.
Chapter 13 Competition: Enhances economic efficiency and consumer welfare by giving each party the right to establish or maintain existing competition laws and the authority to prohibit anti-competition activities, information exchange regulations and Coordination in law enforcement activities.
Chapter 14: Small and Medium Enterprises: Promote the implementation of economic cooperation programs and economic activities to increase the capacity of small and medium enterprises to utilize and take advantage of opportunities from the ASEAN Agreement, especially Integration into regional supply chains and so on.
Chapter 15: Economic and Technical Cooperation: Focuses on narrowing the development gap through technical assistance programs and building capacity to support the effective implementation and implementation of this agreement, giving priority to least developed countries. .
Chapter 16 Government Procurement: Promotes transparency in laws, regulations and procedures and enhances cooperation between all parties in government procurement.
Chapter 17 General Provisions and Exemptions: Defines the obligations of all parties involved in the publication of information, administrative procedures, enforcement of laws, regulations, procedures, as well as the implementation of administrative decisions, oversight And the appeal of the disclosure of information and confidential information by all parties to the Asean Agreement may retain the right to control matters relating to the public interest.
Chapter 18 Institutional Provisions: Establish institutional arrangements for the Asean Agreement and the structure for the Asean Ministers, the Asean Joint Committee and other committees / sub-committees. The ASEAN Joint Committee will review and guide the implementation of the ASEAN Agreement.
Chapter 19 Dispute Resolution: Establish an effective, efficient and transparent mechanism for consulting and resolving disputes arising out of the implementation of this ASEAN Agreement.
Chapter 20: Final Provisions: Defines general reviews and procedures for the amendment of agreements, provisions on entry into force, withdrawal from agreements and accession to the ASEAN Agreement.
