IMF cuts Asian growth forecast as China slows
TOKYO (Reuters) - The International Monetary Fund (IMF) on Friday cut its forecast for Asia's economy as global monetary tightening tightened, war-torn inflation in Ukraine and the sharp fall in tensions. China's economic growth has hurt the outlook for the region's economic recovery.
Reuters reports, citing the IMF's Asia-Pacific Economic Outlook, that while inflation in Asia remains sluggish compared to other regions, most central banks must continue to raise interest rates to ensure they remain stable. In anticipation.
The IMF cut its growth forecast for Asia to 4 percent this year and 4.3 percent next year, down 0.9 percentage points and 0.8 percentage points from its April forecast. The slowdown follows a 6.5 percent increase in 2021.
Among the biggest challenges was China's rapid and widespread economic slowdown due to austerity measures and the country's deteriorating real estate sector, the IMF said.
The IMF expects China's economic growth to slow to 3.2 percent this year, down 1.2 percentage points from its April forecast of 8.1 percent in 2021. . The world's second-largest economy is projected to grow 4.4 percent next year and 4.5 percent by 2024. According to Reuters.
