On Thursday, the administration of US President Joe Biden released a total of three Defense Department documents, including a defense strategy, a nuclear weapons assessment and a missile defense capability assessment. Together, the three documents set out various US military priorities for the coming years.
During the administration of former President Donald Trump, the US military decided in 2018 to develop a new surface-to-surface cruise missile from a warship that could be equipped with a nuclear warhead. Threats from Russia.
The Biden administration, meanwhile, said in its assessment that the SLCM-N missile development program was no longer necessary and would be scrapped because the United States had sufficient resources to deter the enemy. Use of nuclear weapons. Today, the three core means for the use of US nuclear weapons are by land-based fire, by dropping aircraft, and by submarine fire.
However, the Biden administration retains another weapons program of the former Trump administration: the development of ballistic missiles fired from submarines and equipped with nuclear warheads called W76-2. The low-level blast, which the US Department of Defense began deploying in 2020, was in preparation for the deployment of Russia's similarly powerful tactical nuclear weapons.
In the face of the unfavorable situation in the Ukraine war, Russian President Vladimir Putin has repeatedly spoken out about the use of nuclear weapons to protect Russian territory, but US officials claim that so far they have not observed. See any clues that Moscow is preparing to use nuclear weapons.
