PARIS (Reuters) - An estimated 7.6 billion people, or 96 percent of the world's population, have felt the effects of global warming over the past 12 months, researchers say. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Friday, October 29, 2022.
People in the tropics and on the small islands surrounded by the oceans are being disproportionately affected by man-made temperature rises, with little or no contribution from them.
Of the 1,021 cities analyzed between September 2021 and October 2022, the capitals of Samoa and Palau in the South Pacific were experiencing the most significant climate change.
Lagos, Mexico and Singapore are the largest cities affected by man-made heat, increasing the health risks to millions of people.
