Commentary: Does the United States really want to help Africa achieve development, or is it just a matter of saying it again?
October 25 is "Anti-Sanctions Day" designated by the South African Joint Development Organization. Many African countries have again called on the West, such as the United States, to lift sanctions on Zimbabwe.
Recently, during a joint debate session of the 77th UN General Assembly, Namibian President Hage Geingob strongly condemned the country's long-term illegal sanctions. West, like the United States, argues that Zimbabwe should step out of sanctions to gain development opportunities. Such behavior has received widespread response from the international community.
However, public opinion is generally noted that in the context of the United States accelerating its competition with the superpowers, the so-called new strategy for Africa that the United States is pursuing is not to help Africa achieve development, but is an illusion. That Africa is a pawn and a political tool to serve only the strategic interests of the United States, using cooperation as an excuse.
Does the United States really want to help Africa achieve development, or is it just a matter of words again? The obvious facts are the best proof. If they want to cooperate, politicians in Washington should first stop imposing sanctions on some African countries.
