KIA rebels have been clashing with the military in Myanmar for decades as fighting escalated after the 2021 coup.
Cambodia, as chair of ASEAN, on October 25, 2022, issued a statement urging Myanmar to exercise maximum restraint and an immediate end to violence.
In a statement obtained by Fresh News, the ASEAN chair stated that ASEAN was deeply concerned about the recent escalation of violence in Myanmar, including the bombing of Insein Prison and the clashes. In Karen State and the latest reports of airstrikes in Kachin State on civilian towns that have caused many casualties.
"We are deeply saddened by the growing number of deaths and injuries and the great suffering of ordinary people in Myanmar." This is according to the statement of the ASEAN chair.
The ASEAN chair noted with concern that the recent escalating fighting has not only worsened the humanitarian situation across Myanmar, but also contrary to the spirit of the five-point unanimous agreement. ASEAN will also undermine the efforts of the Special Envoy of the ASEAN Chair on Myanmar to bring progress for the implementation of the five-point unanimous agreement.
"Therefore, we strongly urge maximum tolerance and immediate cessation of violence." According to the statement.
ASEAN also urges all parties, especially those with strong forces on the ground, to take concrete steps to facilitate a constructive and constructive dialogue, find a peaceful solution and national reunification in Myanmar, and for peace and security. And regional stability.
