China's economy grows faster than expected in Q3, but recovery is bleak
BEIJING (Reuters) - China's economy rebounded faster than expected in the third quarter, but a stronger recovery in the long run will face measures to curb Kovid-19. The continued decline of the real estate sector and the risk of falling into a recession of the global economy. According to Reuters.
With the help of government measures, the world's second-largest economy grew 3.9 percent in the third quarter from a year earlier, Reuters reported citing official data released on Monday. Last year. That was higher than the 3.4 percent increase in Reuters polls and higher than the 0.4 percent increase in the second quarter.
However, domestic demand slowed until the end of the third quarter due to the outbreak of the quake epidemic, which led to a shutdown as export growth slowed and key real estate sectors fell. Further down, indicating alarming economic recovery.
With a dark outlook, China looks set to move forward with its toughest Kovid policy, backed by the ruling Communist Party, which ended its overthrow on Sunday. And the continuation of President Xi Jinping's third term.
The Bank of Thailand closely monitors the baht and finds no abnormal capital flows
BANGKOK: The Bank of Thailand said last weekend that it was closely monitoring the baht's depreciation amid global financial fluctuations, but there were no signs of unusual capital movements in the country. No, not at all. According to Reuters.
Reuters reports, citing a message from the Central Bank of Thailand sent to reporters after the recent sharp depreciation of the baht, that the devaluation of the Thai currency was mainly driven by Rising US dollar.
The baht depreciated to a 16-year low against the dollar. The currency has depreciated 13 percent against the dollar so far this year.
She said foreign investors had still bought about 110 billion baht ($ 2.9 billion) of Thai assets so far this year, with more than 140 billion baht in net purchases and sales. Net of 30 billion baht in bonds.
Ms Chayawadee added that the Bank of Thailand was urging the private sector to manage currency risk to mitigate the impact of financial market volatility while uncertainty remained high. According to Reuters.
(Pursat): A farmer in Sangkat Chamroeun Phal, Pursat City - was very happy after meeting and gaining farming knowledge from agricultural officials. Happy because he expects that this knowledge will help make his farming more productive and profitable from this farming business.
On October 24, 2022, Mr. Ngin Chhay, a Royal Government delegate in charge of the Director General of Agriculture of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, visited Chamroeun Phal Sangkat, Pursat City, an area with a lot of rice farming. Visit to find out the impact of farmers due to the amount of water and also study the difficulties of farmers on farming.
In a meeting with about 20 farmers, Mr. Ngin Chhay asked and listened to the difficulties as well as some of the challenges of farmers, especially the less profitable farming.
Farmers' issues raised at the time included high prices of fertilizers and pesticides, red snails, low yields and buyers lowering rice prices.
After listening to the problems of farmers, Mr. Ngin Chhay shared and provided some knowledge to the people about sowing rice, using fertilizers, using pesticides and killing red snails.
After listening to the sharing of knowledge, the farmer expressed his happiness and realized that the techniques he had used in the past were not in line with agricultural techniques.
Farming for 3 years, this farmer has never met with agriculture officials to share knowledge on this agricultural regime. Therefore, this sharing made the farmers in Chamroeun Phal very happy and also called on the Ministry of Agriculture to expand the sharing of agricultural knowledge more widely so that farmers have knowledge and do not go wrong. Profits from agriculture.
