Article by Prof. Dr. Nak Chendarith, Director of the Cambodian Center for 21st Century Maritime Silk Road Research at the Royal University of Phnom Penh, Angkor Hospital for Children, Siem Reap Province, launches fundraising campaign to save the lives of Cambodian children from cancer
(Siem Reap): The Angkor Hospital for Children of Siem Reap Province on October 24, 2022 held a press conference to announce the launch of a fundraising campaign to save the lives of Cambodian children from cancer. This campaign runs from October 24, 2022 to December 12, 2022.
The campaign aims to raise and raise funds for the first time, focusing on cancer that will be difficult to treat globally. Through this program, the hospital expects to be able to raise about $ 100,000, as well as the participation of famous actors or singers, business companies, local and foreign philanthropists.
"Cancer is a disease that can affect anyone, including children," said Dr. Samly Vannak, a cancer specialist. Cancer occurs when cells in the body metabolize uncontrollably and spread to other organs. In Cambodia, the number of cancer cases is increasing significantly.
Dr. Khov Phara, Head of Ophthalmology, said that as an ophthalmologist at Angkor Hospital for Children, he has treated many children with cataracts. Most parents are shocked to learn that their child has cataract cancer. Ophthalmologists and oncologists work closely together to treat each child with cancer.
Meanwhile, Dr. Nguon Chanpheaktra, Director of Angkor Hospital for Children, said that in order to provide cancer screening and care services as a whole, it costs between $ 350,000 and $ 450,000 per year. Without adequate funding, cancer screening and care may be reduced or face closure, affecting the lives of many Cambodian children who are receiving services from Angkor Hospital for Children.
Dr. Nguon Chanpheaktra also called for the support of this program, please contribute to the monthly or annual donation to Angkor Hospital for Children and share this call to friends and family, you can save lives Children in the present and in the future.
Please note that Angkor Hospital for Children opened the oncology department in 2012 to examine, treat and care for this deadly disease, while the treatment of cataract cancer has also begun. In 2021, the hospital also launched a separate cancer-focused building, treating 508 children with cancer. Three-quarters (three-quarters) of cancer patients have recovered and are still receiving treatment.
