Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that if the Cambodian side does not reach an agreement, there will be no Paris Peace Agreement.
"Negotiations gradually expanded until the first and second informal Jakarta informal meetings, followed by the 1989 Paris Conference," he said. At that time, the five largest countries, which are permanent members of the United Nations, have not yet intervened. It was not until 1990 that the five countries joined in, taking into account the framework of consensus reached by the Cambodian side, plus all the initiatives that major countries must guarantee.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that what Cambodia wanted from the Paris Agreement was an end to the flames of war, but the United Nations could not help. The United Nations spent more than $ 2 billion, but left the Khmer Rouge divided, especially the Khmer Rouge continued to oppose the government. Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that only this division was completely unified through his win-win policy launched in 1996.
