According to the press release, on October 20-22, 2022, the General Department of Immigration deported 263 foreigners, 26 females of 4 nationalities, including 2 Bangladeshis and 16 Thais. 171 Vietnamese nationals and 74 Chinese nationals left the Kingdom of Cambodia in accordance with the Immigration Law at the Phnom Penh International Airport Gate Police Station and the Bavet and Trapeang Phlong International Border Crossing Police Stations.
The above 263 foreigners were arrested in connection with the crime of illegal border crossing, without a passport, stealing, staying, working illegally, in violation of the law on immigration, to be evicted according to the Prakas of the Ministry of Interior and prohibited Entry into Cambodia for 3 years.
Joint study: In 10 years, raccoons and wild boars in the eastern landscape of Cambodia lost a lot
The decline is due to the demand for wildlife meat, which is a major driver of hunting, trapping and illegal wildlife trade. It also increases the risk of animal-to-human transmission of the disease, posing a serious threat to the environment and public health.
A report by the Mondulkiri Mobile Law Enforcement Team shows that in the last 18 months from 2021, a total of 1,353 kilograms of wildlife meat was confiscated while in circulation. Trading market. At the same time, law enforcement officers in Stung Treng and Ratanakkiri provinces confiscated a total of more than 200 kilograms of wildlife meat during raids on markets in the last six months.
According to Neth Pheakka, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, "Eating wild animals for health or to cure various diseases, as well as for strength or self-esteem, is a complete misunderstanding." "On the contrary, eating wild animals not only poses a risk to the health of those who eat them, but unfortunately it also encourages hunting, trapping and trafficking in wildlife of universal value."
Mr. Seng Teak, President of WWF Cambodia, said: “Global value animals such as big tigers and wild oxen have become extinct due to the trapping, poaching and illegal trade that these activities are taking place. "Continue to kill wildlife on a regular basis, causing the number of other wildlife in the protected area to plummet."
The cluster study highlighted: "Wildlife affected by demand for wildlife meat and illegal trade, including toenails, such as oxen, wild boars, deer, deer, as well as vultures. "
Mr. Seng Teak added: "The understanding of buyers, sellers and traders about the health risks through eating and trading wildlife is very important to enable them to make a commitment to stop buying, selling, trading and eating. .
Mr. Ou Sopheakdey, Director of Mondulkiri Forestry Administration, emphasized: "This is a time when we urgently need to work together to develop and implement innovative approaches to address the root causes of the decline and trade in wildlife." And together implement the program to restore wildlife and nature.
