(Mondulkiri): Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Environment, said that in the 18 months for 2021-2022, officials have cracked down on 1,353 kilograms of wildlife meat in Mondulkiri province.
Mr. Net Pheaktra made this statement during a press conference at the Mondulkiri Provincial Department of Environment on the Zero Trap Campaign: Stop Wildlife Trafficking this morning, October 22, 2022.
Speaking at a press conference, a senior official of the Ministry of Environment stated: "For 18 months in 20221 to 2022, that is, 12 months in 2021 and 6 months in 2022, the authorities have cracked down on wildlife trade until 1,353 kg in Mondulkiri province. In the past six months, Mondulkiri province has also cracked down on 235 kilograms of wildlife trade.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra added: "If you count the number of crackdowns and prevention, the relevant authorities are more active than before. This action took place after the start of the Zero Trap campaign, which the Ministry of Environment and its partners have launched in the past.
On that occasion, Mr. Net Pheaktra also called on people to stop eating wild animals and called on restaurants to stop selling wild animals, please do not enter the forest to kill wild animals to stop immediately. Because these acts are illegal, it is an act that society does not recognize at all.
"In the past, those who posted the killings have been criticized and hated, and some of them have come to their senses and are determined to stop such activities," he said.
In addition, he stated that Cambodians now have a better understanding of wildlife protection, know the value of wildlife and know the value of wildlife in attracting tourism, which provides income to the community and the Cambodian people do not know how to dry. .
According to Mr. Chan Ratana, Deputy Governor of Mondulkiri Province, the authorities have strongly prevented and suppressed wildlife trade in order to preserve wildlife in Mondulkiri. Relevant officials in all departments not only prevent and suppress wildlife trafficking, but also vigorously maintain wildlife sanctuaries almost everywhere.
It should be noted that the wildlife trafficking campaign is part of the zero trap campaign that the Ministry of Environment and its partners have successfully implemented in the past 6 provinces.
