Vietnam asks central bank to facilitate funding for fuel imports
HANOI: Vietnam's government has asked its central bank to help local oil companies have better access to foreign currency to pay for fuel imports, the Ministry of Industry and Trade said on Wednesday. .
The VN Express reported, citing a statement issued by the Ministry of Industry and Trade, that facilitating access to the fund would help oil companies "buy their fuel products quickly for the domestic market."
The statement came days after gas stations in southern Vietnam closed or restricted their sales, citing financial difficulties, according to Vietnamese state media.
Southeast Asian countries this year have faced a sharp rise in the price of imported fuel and a rise in the value of the US dollar, making fuel imports more expensive.
Malaysia joined other OPEC + members this week in support of a drastic reduction in OPEC + oil output targets after the White House accused Saudi Arabia of forcing some countries to support the measure.
Reuters reports, quoting Malaysian Economy Minister Mustapa Mohamed in a statement issued on Tuesday: "OPEC + countries have jointly considered factors including the fundamentals of "Markets in particular to address global oil supply uncertainty and demand conditions."
"Considering the vision of long-term uncertainty, Malaysia will continue our close cooperation with OPEC + to ensure the stability of the global oil market," he said.
Tomorrow morning, Samdech Techo Hun Sen meets for the third time with people volunteering to move construction out of the Angkor resort area
(Siem Reap): On the morning of Friday, October 21, 2022, tomorrow, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, will continue to meet for the third time with about 1,853 families who have volunteered to relocate their houses and structures. From the Angkor resort area and the area under the control of the APSARA Authority to settle in the Run Ta Ek development area.
The meeting will take place at Run Ta Ek Natural Village in Banteay Srei District, Siem Reap Province, where the Royal Government provides land to people in exchange for the demolition of buildings from the Angkor resort area and the area controlled by the APSARA Authority.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen has already met with residents volunteering to move out of the Angkor resort area and the area controlled by the Apsara Authority twice. The first one was held on September 13 with the participation of about 1,117 families. The second one was held on October 1, with the participation of about 2,751 families.
At the same time, people volunteered to demolish illegal constructions from Angkor and other areas under the control of APSARA Authority went to select a total of 9,323 families, including 5,823 families to the Run Ta Ek development site. And 3,200 families to the development site.
Currently, the work of land preparation and construction of infrastructure is assigned by Samdech Techo Prime Minister to Samdech Techo Hun Sen Siem Reap Engineering Corps, which is under the direct command of General Kheng Someth. In order to fulfill Samdech Techo's order on the construction of infrastructure and the clearing of forests and land for the people, General Kheng Someth took the lead and commanded the force, and the engineering team worked without a break.
According to the report, the construction of infrastructure in the Run Ta Ek development area is gaining momentum and is emerging in large numbers.
The people expressed their gratitude for the thought and attention of Samdech Techo, the head of the Royal Government, in organizing the village and making it easier for them to live.
Many families who have already seized the plots have started to come down and prepare the land to build houses and grow various crops on their land. Residents have all expressed their satisfaction with coming to live in Run Ta Ek village because they have legally owned land for their children and can arrange to build houses as they wish. Far from living in the Angkor resort area, who do not have the right to own land and can not manage to build a house according to their wishes.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, in a meeting with volunteers to live in Run Ta Ek village, expressed his commitment to turn Run Ta Ek village from a forest area into a development area and intends to make Run Ta Ek village into a town.
