Senior Minister Osman Hassan Invited to Speak at Global Muslim Business Forum 2022
The Global Muslim Business Forum aims to build the success of the Muslim world and focus on promoting strategic partnerships, dialogue and business cooperation to better economic recovery for common prosperity and sustainable development. The global economy has been hit hard by the Kovid-19 pandemic.
Speaking at the forum, Dr. Osman Hassan said that in terms of international trade volume, Cambodia exported about 12.6 billion US dollars in 2018 and 19.3 billion US dollars in 2021. Only 10 percent of Cambodia's trade volume with Islamic countries. While Cambodia's import trade volume is expected to reach 17.3 billion in 2018 and 28.7 billion in 2021.
He added that Cambodia is a non-Muslim country with good relations with member countries of the Organization of Islamic Cooperation (OIC) and Cambodia aims to increase its trade volume to 25% of Cambodia's total trade. .
Cambodia is currently also producing halal products, which have been under the jurisdiction of the Cambodian Minister of Commerce since 2008. The Halal Institution of Cambodia, led by the Director of the Cambodian Islamic Council, is trained and accredited by JAKIM Malaysia.
Currently, Halal Cambodia has issued certificates to 227 companies, 27 restaurants, 94 industries and 852 products, and the list is growing. These are the products that Cambodia will export to the markets of Islamic and non-Muslim countries in the world.
He also said that the next thing the Cambodian Muslim community needs to do is Islamic financing. If we have an Islamic financing institution, it will complement the chain of growing Cambodian halal products.
He stressed that Islamic financing is in high demand, which will be an effective commercial investment for the nation and for the entire Muslim community. We welcome any party wishing to invest in Islamic financing in Cambodia, and government support is available at any time.
According to the Global Islamic Economic Report 2020, more than 1.9 billion Muslims have spent more than $ 2.02 trillion on six economies led by halal modern food. Islamic financial assets are estimated at $ 2.88 trillion in 2019 and Muslims are expected to spend about $ 2.4 trillion by 2024.
