Sri Lanka passes law allowing oil companies to import oil
(Colombo): Sri Lanka on Tuesday passed a law to allow companies from oil-producing countries to import and sell fuel in their country as the country struggles to cope with the huge fuel costs that have caused the crisis. Its finances are getting worse. According to Reuters.
The island nation of 22 million has a severe shortage of foreign exchange reserves, making it difficult to import enough essential materials, including oil, fertilizers and food.
He added that Sri Lanka's monthly fuel costs have doubled to $ 600 million this year, partly due to global warming due to the war in Ukraine, but the country could only afford about 350 million. Dollars only.
The minister said the state-owned bank was also struggling to repay about $ 751 million in debt owed to suppliers.
The new law will liberalize the fuel industry, allowing international companies to import and sell fuel directly in Sri Lanka and reduce the country's reliance on a small dollar supply.
Reuters quoted a source from the Sri Lankan Ministry of Energy as saying that the ministry had already received 24 applications from India, China, Saudi Arabia, Russia, Malaysia, the United Kingdom and the United States.
