Turkish parliament approves jail term for journalist or social media user for up to three years
TURKEY: Turkey's parliament on Thursday passed a bill proposed by President Tayyip Erdogan that would imprison journalists and social media users for up to three years for spreading false information. Great concern about freedom of expression. According to a release by the AFP news agency.
Article 29 of the law raises serious concerns over free speech, emphasizing that those who spread false information online about Turkey's security in order to create fear and disrupt public order will face Imprisonment for 1 to 3 years.
European observers have noted that the new law is not intended to eliminate "false or misleading information" but to pave the way for another crackdown on dissidents in Turkey.
