Speaking to Fresh News on October 18, 2022, Mr. Bun Barang said that with the attention of the relevant ministries as well as the Royal Government has given him the confidence to import salt products from abroad. Supply to the local market demand and fill the shortage of salt for the people to eat in 2022 and beyond.
Mr. Bun Barang said that in Kampot-Kep province in recent years, salt production has not benefited due to rainfall in almost all seasons. Especially this year, salt production was affected because the people who produced the salt did not benefit from the production at all.
He said he had asked the government to start importing salt for the first time in early August 2022 and now imports more than 50,000 tonnes for Cambodia, from India.
Mr. Bun Barang said that the salt imported from India had arrived at the port of Teuk Sap in Leuk Dek district, Kandal province. Initially, there were about 60,000 tons and this time, more than 50,000 tons were imported.
Mr. Bun Barang said that the import of salt from India will continue to meet the demand for salt in Cambodia to eat more than 100,000 tons per year. He said that at present, the production of salt in the country does not produce salt for sale due to the rainy weather.
The import of salt from India does not affect the health of the people, because the salt products in India are not only imported to Cambodia, but some countries around the world that have a shortage of salt is also imported from India. Also, because salt production in India comes from nature, it is very good quality compared to both the tropical climate and the Indian Ocean. This is the additional confirmation of Mr. Bun Barang.
He added that the import of salt to the country in the port above is closely monitored by the relevant ministries, especially the Ministry of Finance and the Ministry of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation by assigning the Ministry's expert team to inspect. Regarding salt, the Ministry states that the salt imported from India is 100% free of chemical additives.
He also added that neighboring countries such as Vietnam, we can not import to Cambodia because Vietnam lacks salt like Cambodia. As for the cost of importing salt from India to distribute in the Cambodian market now, the normal price should not be higher than the price of our domestically produced salt, although the price of fuel, labor costs, labor costs, and other taxes are included. Anyway.
Mr. Bun Barang said that the import of salt is based on the shortage of salt in Cambodia, not to exceed and not to lack is enough demand for consumption in Cambodia, while the salt in Cambodia can not produce salt. Sufficient harvest due to heavy rains almost all seasons, especially salt-producing provinces such as Kampot-Kep.
Mr. Bun Barang continued that up to now, due to the weather this year, there was heavy rain, making nearly 5,000 hectares of salt fields in Kampot and Kep provinces can not produce salt at all.
It should also be noted that in August 2022, Cambodia began to import 60,000 tons of salt from India for the first time to meet domestic demand. This is the second time that Cambodia has imported salt from India. Phnom Penh Capital Administration is the first sub-national administration to use the public spending appraisal system and financial accountability
(Phnom Penh): Phnom Penh Capital Administration is rated as the first sub-national administration to use the Public Expenditure Evaluation and Financial Accountability (PEFA) system, which is an international budget management system and can serve as a model for sub-national institutions. Others to implement this system in order to strengthen the management of the budget system of each state unit more effectively.
Mr. Ros Silva, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Economy and Finance and Secretary General of the General Secretariat of the Public Financial Management Reform Steering Committee, praised the Phnom Penh Capital Administration for implementing this system, which is a positive finding and hopes that the Capital Phnom Penh will play an important role in public financial reform as a model for implementation in other provinces.
He noted that the PEFA framework is widely used by more than 150 countries around the world to evaluate public financial management systems in order to identify the strengths and weaknesses of the system as an input for public financial management reform. Stages in line with the direction of the priority work set, international standards and practices.
Mr. Ros Silva continued, "Based on the results of the PEFA assessment, the sub-national administration for Phnom Penh has been equipped and implemented in the Phnom Penh administration, but the order needs to be further reformed to be in line with the program. "Reform public financial management and, in particular, in line with the strategy of reforming the sub-national administrative budget system 2019-2025."
Mr. Keut Chhe, Deputy Governor of the Board of Governors of Phnom Penh, represented Mr. Khuong Sreng, Governor of Phnom Penh, said that the management of public finances is an important part of the implementation of the reform program that the government has set out to use all the potential and opportunities. Available at both the national and sub-national levels, it is clear, up-to-date, up-to-date, responsive to the real needs of the people, and socio-economic and political developments.
He added that the preparation and dissemination of the Public Expenditure Assessment and Financial Accountability Report (PEFA) for the Phnom Penh Capital Administration is an important part of demonstrating ownership, management, evaluation and use of the assessment results.
