Deputy Prime Minister, Senior Minister and Minister of 7 Institutions Meet to Solve Flood Victims
(Phnom Penh): The Deputy Prime Minister, Senior Minister and Minister from 7 Ministries and Institutions on the morning of October 18, 2022 met to discuss the impact of the flood.
The zoning meeting examined and discussed the assessment of the impact of flood damage on rice and mixed crops due to floods and the establishment of emergency measures.
According to the National Committee for Disaster Management, this year's floods affected many provinces along the Mekong River and around the Tonle Sap Lake. The provinces around the Tonle Sap Lake were the worst affected: Banteay Meanchey, Siem Reap, Battambang, Kampong Thom and Pursat.
A recent report by the National Committee for Disaster Management showed that Banteay Meanchey affected 12,497 families, inundated 31,998 hectares of paddy fields and flooded 2,988 hectares of mixed crops.
Siem Reap province affected 25,023 families and flooded 36,586 hectares of rice.
Pursat province affected 6,676 families and flooded 11,365 hectares of rice.
In response to this impact, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, has issued an order for all relevant officials to urgently study, assess the impact and release rice varieties and other mixed crops to the people to restore cultivation. In particular, ensure the restoration of people's livelihoods.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen also announced the release of a budget package to support people affected by the floods for three months to alleviate difficulties in life.
To speed up the replanting of farmers in the past few days, Senior Minister Kun Kim has already brought a total of more than 600 tons of rice seeds to distribute to people in Banteay Meanchey, Siem Reap, Kampong Thom and Kampong Cham.
Next week, Samdech Techo Hun Sen will visit Banteay Meanchey, Battambang and Pursat provinces to distribute rice seeds directly to people affected by the floods.
