Rising food prices, transportation costs, disruption caused by the Kovit-19 pandemic, ongoing conflicts, including the war in Ukraine, and the rise of climate-related disasters such as floods and droughts are driving Increase food insecurity and malnutrition. The worst affected are vulnerable populations and people who depend on agriculture for their livelihood.
"Because the food system is linked to many other aspects of our lives and our economy, including agriculture, natural resources, energy and health," said Antonio Seavon, Acting Representative of the FAO in Cambodia. Food really has potential and is a means for an equitable and prosperous future. "A sustainable food system will benefit everyone, including the most vulnerable."
According to a recent WFP market update, rice prices, which are the basis of the Cambodian diet, remain stable. However, the prices of nutritious foods that people like to eat, such as duck eggs, vegetables and prawns, increased by 20.8 percent and 30.7 percent year on year. The price of imported food also increased significantly.
