UNICEF said on Monday that children are suffering the most from the economic crisis caused by the war in Ukraine. Conflict and rising inflation have pushed an additional 4 million children across Eastern Europe and Central Asia into poverty, up 19 percent from 2021. This is according to UNICEF's data on data from 22 countries.
Since Moscow launched its offensive, the number of Russian children falling into poverty has risen by about three-quarters, with 2.8 million children living below the poverty line. About half a million Ukrainian children live in poverty.
The problem that drives people into poverty stems from the increasing proportion of income spent on food and fuel, leaving little to be spent on health care and children's education.
