Understand business: Financial experts say raising interest rates will be a safe new investment option for Cambodians
(Phnom Penh): While some banks and microfinance institutions have raised their deposit interest rates, financial experts say this is a good opportunity for Cambodians to invest in deposits at financial institutions. That is to benefit from high interest rates and security, especially for people who have a lot of cash on hand and do not need to use.
He encourages those who have a lot of money left over and do not use it to send it to banks and microfinance institutions for a certain period of time, because in addition to making a profit, it also contributes to the economy. Because through this deposit will make financial institutions have the capital to lend to those who need money to do business or expand their business, which makes the economy grow.
At the same time, depositing money at a fixed term bank will also help reduce their spending on unnecessary purchases. This will make them use their money more efficiently.
He noted that the knowledge of Cambodians related to depositing money is more understandable in both urban and rural areas, with most shifting from depositing money at home to sending it to banks and microfinance.
"Banks and microfinance licensed by the National Bank of Cambodia are the safest place, so people have nothing to worry about," he added. "And this deposit is also a contribution to the economy, we do not put money outside the system that makes cash flow operational."
Recently, some banks and microfinance institutions announced to increase their deposit interest rates, including for riel deposits of more than 8% and more than 7% for dollars. The increase was due to the US Federal Reserve raising interest rates to reduce inflation.
Kaing Tong Ngy, spokesman for the Cambodian Microfinance Association, said that in Cambodia, the safest and most profitable investment is to make deposits at microfinance institutions and banks.
He said that after the announcement of the rate hike, there was a lot of interest from the public.
"At this stage, we have not yet been able to assess the growth of deposits, but we have seen a lot of public interest," he added. "And since October 31 is ASEAN Savings Day, there will be more campaigns to encourage the public to participate in saving at official institutions."
According to the spokesperson of the Cambodian Microfinance Association, compared to last year, the deposit rate increase of a large number of institutions increased between 0.5% and 1%, and in some cases may increase more than 1%. . In general, riel deposits receive higher interest rates than the dollar, thus encouraging Cambodians to use the riel more.
