Dr. Hun Manet said this at the inauguration ceremony of the Great Kantha Kod Temple and achievements in Wat Vetbouvorn in Prek Thmey commune, Koh Thom district, Kandal province, on October 15, 2022.
To support the above concept, Dr. Hun Manet cited the implementation of Samdech Techo Hun Sen's win-win policy as an example.
The future Cambodian leader, who has military, political and economic knowledge, said that Samdech Techo Hun Sen's win-win policy was not to everyone's liking. Some of them gave reasons to destroy the Khmer Rouge without negotiation.
At this point, Dr. Hun Manet highlighted that the founder of win-win politics, Samdech Techo took a clear position to know that some interests must be sacrificed to the Khmer Rouge, but there are no losses.
Dr. Samdech highlighted that "Samdech Techo is clear that we must sacrifice some interests to go to the Khmer Rouge. Some of the territories that he used to control let him continue to rule, and some of the divisions that he controlled the commanders to equip him. It is not a loss for our Khmer, but if we raise an army to destroy him, Khmer, when will the Khmer be finished? ”
It should be noted that the win-win policy was introduced by Samdech Techo Hun Sen at the end of 1996 after he saw that there was only one reconciliation policy to end the war with the Khmer Rouge forces who continued to struggle in the jungle along the border. Thai territory.
Under the three principles of: guaranteeing life, maintaining roles, duties and occupations, and securing property, the Khmer Rouge leaders and the Khmer Rouge army volunteered to coexist with the Royal Government. Samdech Techo Hun Sen designated December 29, 1998 as the time for the successful implementation of the win-win policy and also the time for closing the pages of the history of national division for about 500 years in the history of Cambodia.
A complete end to the flames of war under a win-win policy for the past 24 years has brought peace to Cambodia, bringing great progress and prosperity to the nation and the people of Cambodia as a whole.
