Comment: US trade war with China completely failed, US side needs to absorb lessons
Economic data is a clear indication. According to Moody's research, more than 90 percent of tariffs on US trade wars with China are borne by the United States. According to Bloomberg, raising tariffs on China has significantly boosted spending by US consumers and businesses, and has not boosted U.S. production.
There is no doubt that the lessons that caused the United States to completely fail in the tariff war with China are profound. But how to absorb the lessons is testing the wisdom of Washington politicians.
First of all, they need to be aware that actions contrary to general economic knowledge and market rules are not possible. Second, using so-called "political legitimacy" to confuse policy toward China will not solve its problems. If it is to show "tough attitude towards China" that it does not dare to abolish the tariffs, it is a short-sighted political ploy to destroy the interests of the American state and people.
The past few years, whether inciting "strategic competition" or provoking "all-round opposition", have proved that the US side's understanding of China is seriously flawed. The Chinese side has repeatedly stated that the goal of development is not to surpass or replace the United States, but to constantly strive for self-sufficiency in order to improve the lives of the Chinese people.
China and the United States have good relations and will be able to benefit each other. Suck lessons from failure, correct wrongdoings and only open the door to the future of win-win cooperation.
