IMF warns of slowing economic growth and rising market risk
The International Monetary Fund (IMF) warned earlier this week that curbing inflationary pressures, the energy and food crisis due to war and rising interest rates are pushing the world toward recession and threatening stability. Financial markets. According to Reuters.
The International Monetary Fund (IMF) has urged central banks to maintain their fight against inflation despite the pain caused by tightening monetary policy and a two-decade high of the dollar, Reuters reported. Anyway.
Reducing its 2023 global growth forecast further, the IMF said in a report on its global economic outlook that countries representing one-third of global economic output could fall behind. Economy next year.
The IMF said next year's global gross domestic product (GDP) growth will slow to 2.7 percent from its July forecast of 2.9 percent. Higher interest rates have weakened the US economy, Europe is struggling with rising gas prices, and China faces continued resistance to the Kovid-19 crisis and a slump in the real estate sector. According to Reuters.
