The Japanese yen depreciated the most since 1998, with intervention expected.
The Japanese yen weakened the most against the dollar in 24 years on Wednesday, raising expectations for a second round of intervention. According to Reuters.
The Japanese currency depreciated to 146.35 yen per dollar, the lowest level since August 1998, when the Asian financial crisis hit a record high. Japanese authorities intervened last month to stem the sharp fall in the yen.
The yen traded around 146.30 per dollar on Wednesday afternoon as traders weighed on US inflation data and the impact of inflation on US interest rate hikes. America of the future.
"We are closely monitoring the foreign exchange fluctuations with great caution and are ready to take appropriate action against excessive price fluctuations," Japanese Cabinet Chief Hirokazu Matsuno told Reuters.
