MBCC is initiating a partnership with Wing Bank to conduct additional fundraising campaigns to enable Cambodia to achieve its 2025 demining goals. MBCC proposed this idea during a visit with the Cambodian Mine Action Authority to the mine field in Ang Duch Te village, Srong commune, Kong Pisey district, Kampong Speu province on October 12, 2022.
General Sem Sovanny said there were many mines to be cleared in the country and he appreciated the initiative of the MBCC chairman and his partners. He added that many ideas were initiated by the MBCC and its partners to achieve the mine-state government's mission by 2025.
"All the people in the country are getting the opportunity to help the government clear mines, and every riel is important in a joint effort to clear mines," said General Sem Sovanny.
According to the CMAA, over the past 30 years, nearly 20,000 people have been killed by landmines and nearly 40,000 injured, with more than 9,000 amputated. More than 2,200 square kilometers are covered by landmines or remnants of war, and demining is required to prevent further damage to communities, the economy and society.
"We do not just stop for dinner parties," he said. We will arrange a tour directly from our sponsors. "We will work hard to support the government's efforts to clear mines and return communes and villages." The tycoon emphasizes.
He added that MBBC and partners were also seeking funding for deminers and mine victims. Chevvy, 65, who lives in the village, said she really supports the MBCC initiative since small contributions can make a big difference and completely change their lives. "Even though I do not have enough money, I will support the 4,000 riel campaign because many people have died from landmines," she said.
Meanwhile, Kong Charath, 67, said she would contribute to demining, even if she lacked it. "I'm sure a little encouragement can make a big difference," she said. I want to see the dream of seeing my mine-free village come true. And I know you have the same dreams as me. ”
About 4 to 6 million landmines and other unexploded ordnance (UXO) were dumped across the country after the three decades of war in Cambodia ended in 1998.
