For more than a week now, the crisis between Russia and Ukraine has flared up again after Russian President Putin warned that Russia would use all available weapons, including nuclear weapons, to defeat enemies and defend its territory while forces Russia has also fired dozens of missiles at cities across Ukraine after Putin accused Ukraine of being behind the Crimean bombings.
Dr. Hong Vannak, an economist at the Royal Academy of Cambodia, told Fresh News that the war had taken a toll on Europe as a whole, including the United States and Russia's allies, which would boost the economy. Europe is in crisis and is affecting Asia and Africa.
He added that due to Cambodia's preferential treatment from the European Union, the EBA could affect Cambodia's exports to Europe, especially clothing, footwear, and travel bags, as they reduce the purchase of unnecessary items for tourists. Europe may also cut back on travel.
On this issue, Nget Chou, an economic observer in Cambodia, said that although the war between the two countries did not recur, the global crisis is still protracted due to the uncertainty of the global economic recovery. And the global economic crisis that the world, including Cambodia, is facing will last at least two to three years.
Both economists have suggested that the resurgence of the war will continue to push up fuel prices in Cambodia and other countries as a result of Western sanctions. Russia, a source of gas and oil supplies, and Russia's response to those, as OPEC members have announced cuts in production to push up oil prices.
For Nget Chou, to prepare for the crisis, Cambodia must increase productivity in production by attracting local and foreign investors to invest in the economy to withstand external pressure.
"The influx of foreign investment and the establishment of processing plants are all contributing, and in particular the government can help boost investment in the agricultural processing sector to ensure price stability," he said. "At least at a good price to help deal with this situation. If we rely on exports to Vietnam, Thailand and China, it is still fragile."
At a time when the world, including Cambodia, is facing an economic crisis and rising inflation, the Royal Government of Cambodia recently decided to launch a cash subsidy program for vulnerable families during inflationary pressures. Especially informal economists. The offer will run from November 2022, with nearly 600,000 families, or nearly 2 million people, receiving the subsidy.
