Asia-Pacific air traffic will continue to recover
Sydney: Air passenger traffic in the Asia-Pacific region by the end of this year should recover to about 73% of the 2019 level, up from 53% in August amid restrictions on travel within the country. Is loosened. According to Reuters.
"There is no confusion," Reuters quoted Philip Goh, vice president for Asia-Pacific of the International Air Transport Association (IATA), as saying in an online seminar. "I think the momentum is strong, especially in all major markets in the Asia-Pacific region now except China."
According to IATA data, the recovery in air travel in Asia has been slower than in other parts of the world, largely due to China's ongoing border closures, which have kept the number of passengers low. Its international travel averages only 2% to 3% of pre-epidemic levels.
Goh said air traffic estimates at the end of the year were based on China opening its borders soon, although the exact date remains unclear.
In the positive news for Japan Airlines resumed the resumption of visa-free travel for tourists on Tuesday this week, while Taiwan will end its travel arrangements for travelers this Thursday and Hong Kong. Stalled last month.
According to the IATA, international passenger traffic in the region is at 38 percent of the 2019 level this August. The association estimates that reaching pre-epidemic levels will take place until 2025. According to Reuters, the Thai economy is recovering as tourism returns.
"Our economy depends on tourism, which is slowly recovering," Reuters quoted Arkhom as saying. He added that not all countries are experiencing slowing economic growth and therefore the impact on Thailand's tourism market will be limited.
Tourism is one of the main drivers of Southeast Asia's second largest economy. In 2019, Thailand welcomed nearly 40 million tourists at a cost of 1.91 trillion baht.
The Thai government expects tourism revenue to reach $ 64.5 billion this year from 10 million tourists, Reuters reported.
Mr Arkhom said fiscal and monetary policy would be combined to support economic recovery after the Bank of Thailand raised its benchmark interest rate twice by 0.25% in August. And September.
