Keo Remy: The Establishment of a National Human Rights Institution as a Demonstration of Cambodia's Real Will for Human Rights and International Human Rights Obligations
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Keo Remy, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Chairman of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee, stated that the establishment of the National Human Rights Institution is a testament to the real will of the Royal Government of Cambodia in the field of human rights. International human rights obligations as a member state of the United Nations.
He said that this national human rights body has a very important role to play in promoting and protecting human rights and fundamental freedoms. It will also act as a bridge between the Royal Government and national and international civil society groups on human rights.
According to the President of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee, the Royal Government of Cambodia has clearly expressed its willingness to establish an independent national human rights institution with a role in protecting and promoting human rights in Cambodia by deciding to allow the establishment of a National Committee. Cambodian human rights since 1997, when the Royal Government assigned the representative of NGOs to chair the working group to draft the law.
In addition, Mr. Keo Remy also stated that the Paris Principles of the National Human Rights Institution have 6 main points: 1: broad mandate, 2: autonomy, 3: independence, 4: pluralism, 5: sufficient resources and 6: sufficient power. Today, expert speakers from national human rights institutions in ASEAN countries including the Philippines, Malaysia and Indonesia will explain in more detail some of the principles.
