Comment: The world's largest desert solar power station in the world witnessed China's high-quality new energy development path
Clean energy today accounts for 60 percent of China's growing energy consumption. The restructuring of energy consumption is to fulfill China's promise to reduce carbon emissions by 2030 and carbon neutrality by 2060. This is China's commitment to follow the path of green and low carbon development.
At the same time, the international media and some experts in the field of energy saving and carbon offsets have noted that China's decision is crucial for the world's coal-fired power plants.
According to data released by the US Global Energy Observatory, China's pledge to halt overseas coal-fired power projects means that $ 50,000 million in investment will be lost annually. Can reduce CO2 emissions by 200 million tons.
Some of these new measures make it clear to the international community that China's commitment to authoritative contribution to tackling climate change is not just a matter of words, but of a clear plan. In fact, in recent years, green development has become a key concept that has guided China's economic and social development, and is also a reference point that China has firmly pledged to the outside world.
According to data released by the National Energy Administration of China in July this year, energy consumption per unit of GDP has fallen by 20 percent since 2014, providing significant support for growth. The national economy accounts for 6.2 percent of growth, with consumption growth averaging about 2.9 percent per year. The structure of energy consumption has been significantly improved.
The coal consumption ratio fell from 65.8 percent in 2014 to 56 percent in 2021, averaging 1.4 percent a year, the slowest pace in history. The clean energy consumption ratio rose from 16.9 percent to 25.5 percent over the same period, more than 60 percent of China's energy consumption.
It is clear that in order to achieve the grand goal of responding to climate change, China has drawn up a clear "road map". At the same time, the United States and the West have also announced their respective carbon offsets. These similar promises are active, but what is even more important is to put them into practice.
Resisting climate change is not a geopolitical substitution, and "alternative multilateralism" is not true multilateralism. China's top leaders have called on countries to uphold the international system, which places the United Nations at its core, and adhere to the goals and principles of the "United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change" and the "Paris Agreement".
These documents should be the basic object of coping with global climate change, the West should not draft other agreements. Given the differences in the stages of development, economic capacity and technical means, as a basis for global climate governance, the principles of accountability, as well as differences, need to be further strengthened.
