Samdech Techo Hun Sen Shows Some Legal Aspects to Opposition Lacking Knowledge on Appointment or Removal of Officials
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, presented some legal aspects to the opposition group regarding the appointment or removal of members of the Royal Government.
Regarding the appointment or removal of members of the Royal Government, Samdech Techo instructed the opposition to visit Article 21, Chapter 2 of the Constitution, which states the King, and to read Article 20, Chapter 3 of the Law on the Organization and Functioning of the Council of Ministers.
Samdech Techo stated, "In order to contribute to helping the opposition to have the knowledge that can lead to power in the next nation." "Please refer to Article 21, Chapter 2 of the Constitution, which states about the King. Next, please read Article 20, Chapter 3 of the Law on the Organization and Functioning of the Council of Ministers."
Samdech Techo further stated that due to lack of knowledge, he had just been imprisoned and fled the country and was accused of being a dictator. Samdech Techo told the opposition to try to learn more to reach power in the next world and not to get too involved with the game. Samdech Techo confirmed that this writing is a good intention of Samdech for the ignorant group.
It should be noted that this legal aspect was raised after the opposition group criticized Samdech regarding the removal of Mr. Veng Sakhon from the post of Minister of Agriculture. Opposition parties have stated they will not run in the by-elections.
Article 21 of the Constitution states that at the request of the Council of Ministers, the King shall sign a Royal Decree appointing, changing or terminating the duties of senior civil and military officials, ambassadors and envoys extraordinary and competent.
Separately, Article 20 of the Law on the Organization and Functioning of the Council of Ministers states that the Prime Minister proposes to the King to terminate the position of a member of the Royal Government by Royal Decree.
