(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, on the evening of October 8, 2022, sent a message to his compatriots about the flood situation in Cambodia, which has seriously affected the agricultural sector.
In this message, Samdech has instructed Deputy Prime Minister Aun Porn Moniroth, Minister of Economy and Finance and Acting Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, to assign the Ministry's professional officers to work with the provinces to assess the impact on agriculture. Due to the flood, we are ready to transport rice seeds to solve the problem.
In a voice message sent to compatriots received by Fresh News on the evening of October 8, 2022, Samdech Techo Hun Sen confirmed that he had received a report from General Kun Kim, First Vice Chairman of the National Committee. Disaster management about the flood situation in Cambodia, which seriously affects the agricultural sector.
The report confirmed that four people had died in the floods and that evacuations had been carried out to safer areas, including those affected by the floods. According to the report, 151,412 hectares of rice fields in Cambodia were flooded and 4,124 hectares of other mixed crops were flooded.
Samdech said that now the water in some places is low, in some places it is rising and there are some situations of the weather forecast that there may be some more rain, but in reality, some places are rising, some places are falling and the situation At the base is manageable. But what is worrying is that some bases are facing problems related to the lack of rice and crop varieties that require immediate national intervention.
Samdech considered that this is a necessary intervention to solve the problem at the end of the season when the water has not run out yet. He called for cooperation between the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, sub-national authorities as well as the Disaster Management Committee to jointly assess the impact and be prepared to transport rice seeds and crops to destinations.
Samdech does not want to allow the water to recede too much and those areas have no water source to grow crops, which Cambodia has experienced in 2000, and the most common provinces are Banteay Meanchey, Battambang, Pursat, and this year was born in Kampong Thom Preah Vihear and other provinces.
Samdech urges the management of this work immediately to assess the damage and prepare to release the stock to the people to grow.
