Rubber prices fluctuated sharply in the second quarter of this year, when prices rebounded in June and then fell sharply in July.
Vietnam News reports that the market has been hit hard by the epidemic, the political crisis, the lack of containers, high shipping costs, the sharp rise in fuel prices and the slow processing of customs clearance, which has affected the global supply chain. Global rubber prices continued to fall in September on concerns about declining demand in China.
Rubber prices are often negatively correlated with the appreciation of the dollar. The rising trend of the US dollar in recent years also predicts that rubber prices will remain low in the near future.
Due to price fluctuations, especially fluctuations, the Chinese market is currently reducing the amount of rubber imported from Vietnam. Therefore, rubber exporters to this market expect their profits to be significantly reduced in the last three months.
A representative from the company explained that the drop in profits was due to lower consumption of rubber, which led to a drop in sales of semi-finished products in the third quarter compared to the same period last year. .
