UNICEF: More than 1 million people have been forced to flee the war in Myanmar since last year's coup
The Southeast Asian nation has been in turmoil since the military overthrew Aung San Suu Kyi's government last year, sparking widespread armed conflict.
The junta has responded with a crackdown that rights groups say has included the destruction of villages, extrajudicial killings and airstrikes on civilians.
UNICEF said in a statement on Thursday that since the coup last month, 1,017,000 people have been displaced from inside the country.
More than half of those forced to flee were in the northwestern region of Sagaing, which saw the heaviest fighting, the agency added.
