Southeast Asia needs to diversify energy as demand picks up
(Jakarta): Domestic energy demand in Southeast Asia could triple by 2050, and the region could become an importer of natural gas and pure coal before 2050 without developing alternative energy sources. According to Reuters.
Reuters quoted a study by the Southeast Asian Energy Agency as finding that for the 10 member states of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN), energy resource diversification would be important not only. Due to efforts to reduce carbon emissions, but also to meet growing energy needs.
According to a recent study by the ACE, rapid economic growth in the region will boost total energy consumption to about 1.28 billion tonnes of oil equivalent by 2050 under the current scenario.
At the same time, without significant discoveries or diversification, Southeast Asia could become a net importer of natural gas by 2025 and an importer of pure coal by 2039. Southeast Asia is home to the world's largest exporter of thermal coal, Indonesia.
Reuters reports that policy intervention is needed to accelerate the development of renewable and alternative energy in the region.
Without policy intervention, ASEAN's share of renewable energy will be only 14.4% of total primary energy supply by 2025, up slightly from 14.2% in 2020 and below the ASEAN target. With 23 percent.
"We need to consider installing about 4 gigawatts (capacity) of nuclear power plants while developing infrastructure for sources such as hydropower and geothermal energy, which the region has the second largest potential," said Nuki.
Southeast Asia should also develop energy storage facilities to optimize future use of solar and wind energy.
For fuel in the transport sector, he said Southeast Asia should increase biofuel and bioethanol consumption and increase electric vehicle consumption by 20 percent by 2050 to reduce fossil fuel consumption. According to Reuters.
