An official from the Phnom Penh Municipal Court told Fresh News that in the defamation case, the trial judge ruled as follows:
Case 1: NEC Complaint against Mr. Son Chhay Judge: 1: Convicted of Son Chhay, fined 9 million Riel for public defamation. 2: Convicted of additional charges against the accused. As follows: 2.1: Posting the sentencing decision at the residence of the accused at the Sangkat Hall of the residence of the accused at the commune / sangkat polling station of the Capital-Province and at the information booth of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court for 2 months.
2.2: Publish the sentencing decision in the press for 8 days. The above costs are borne by the inmate.
2.3: Broadcast the sentencing decision by all means, telecommunication, audio-visual for 8 days. The above costs are borne by the inmate.
2.3: Broadcast the sentencing decision by all means of audio-visual television for 8 days. The above costs are borne by the inmate.
3. Order Son Chhay to pay 3,000 million Riels in compensation to the Civil Party.
The two defamation cases were filed by the National Election Committee and the CPP after Son Chhay alleged that the 2022 5th Commune / Sangkat Council Election There was vote buying and fraud.
In both cases, Son Chhay, represented by the Phnom Penh Municipal Court, was charged with "public defamation" by the National Election Committee (NEC) and the CPP. Cambodians.
In a complaint filed with the Phnom Penh Municipal Court on June 14, 2022, CPP lawyer Ky Tech alleges that Son Chhay's unsubstantiated allegations in previous interviews with the media That is a dishonest act, distorting the truth, giving false information with the intention of blaming the CPP in good faith.
Ky Tech's lawyer asked the Phnom Penh Municipal Prosecutor's Office to investigate, prosecute and convict Son Chhay in accordance with the law and demand 4 billion riels in compensation for damages to the CPP.
As for the NEC complaint, the NEC asked the court to convict Son Chhay for public defamation under Article 305 of the Criminal Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia and to demand that Son Chhay write a letter of apology to the NEC. NEC in writing and publicly available. Ministry of Justice Spokesman: Congestion in some courts due to inadequate out-of-court dispute resolution mechanisms (Video inside)
(Phnom Penh): Through the campaign to resolve the congestion of criminal cases in the Capital-Provincial Courts for 1 year and 1 month, which was closed in the past, the Ministry of Justice found that some cases in the courts. Congested due to insufficient, out-of-court dispute resolution mechanisms, so some minor issues have been pushed to the judiciary.
This is according to Mr. Chin Malin, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Justice in the press conference of the Spokesperson Unit of the Royal Government on the morning of October 7, 2022 under the theme "Achievements for 5 years. Ministry of Justice.
Mr. Jean Malin claimed that although the campaign to resolve the congestion of criminal cases in the capital-provincial courts has been completed, but the principles of the measures that have been put in the past, including measures to implement non-prison sentences. Out-of-court mediation is still being pursued for the sake of justice.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Justice said: "Our campaign cannot be launched for a long time and will last indefinitely, because the campaign is a temporary plane to face real problems Through this campaign, we find "We see that some cases are congested due to insufficient mediation and out-of-court dispute resolution mechanisms, so all the minor issues have to go to court."
According to Mr. Jean Malin, the congestion in this case is also related to the court's limited resources and physical infrastructure, so starting from this campaign, the Ministry has organized a reform to create resources, both financial and physical infrastructure to respond. To the growth of criminal cases that have occurred.
At the same time, Mr. Nak Ser Kirin, Undersecretary of State of the Ministry of Justice, said that the overall results of the congestion criminal case campaign that has been conducted in the capital-provincial courts throughout the country, both the prosecution, the investigation and the trial. A total of more than 37,900 cases, equivalent to 96% of the criminal case, jammed a total of more than 39,500 cases.
He added that more than 12,600 cases have been tried, accounting for 97% of the total of more than 13,000 cases. In addition, the Capital-Provincial Courts throughout the country also handled more than 62,800 new criminal cases, equivalent to 78% of the total number of new cases of more than 80,400 cases.
During the campaign period, a number of additional criminal procedures were implemented that could help alleviate overcrowding in prisons, such as: Deciding not to be remanded in custody and not subject to judicial review; Second: Issuance of Closing Order, Third: Decision to acquit, Fourth: Ordinary sentence, Fifth: Suspension of probation, and Sixth: Release of convicts. Etc.
On May 18, 2020, Mr. Keut Rith, Minister of Justice and Chairman of the CJCC Officially announced the launch of the campaign after several internal meetings of the Ministry and meetings with other stakeholders to ensure the effective implementation of the above campaign. On June 28, 2021, CCHR Announced the closure of the campaign after achieving a better achievement should be proud of the planned goals.
An agreement between the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) and its allies, including Russia, jointly called OPEC +, has agreed to reduce oil supplies in already tense markets. Add to the increase in inflation.
As production in some OPEC + countries is below target, the actual reduction will be less than 2 million barrels per day agreed at the meeting.
Reuters quoted Saudi Energy Minister Prince Abdulaziz bin Salman as saying that the actual supply cut would be around 1 million to 1.2 million barrels a day, in response. To the rise in interest rates in the West and the global economic downturn.
WTO economists also said they now expect global commodity trade to grow 3.5 percent this year, slightly higher than previously expected. However, for 2023, global trade volume will increase by only 1 percent, down sharply from the April forecast of 3.4 percent.
According to the WTO, rising energy prices in Europe as a result of the war in Ukraine are expected to reduce household spending and increase production costs on the continent. Meanwhile, tightening monetary policy in the United States is hurting the housing, auto and investment sectors, and China is still facing an outbreak of Kovid-19 and production disruptions.
The WTO said rising spending on imports of fuel, food and fertilizer faces risks leading to greater food insecurity and debt problems in developing countries. If this forecast comes true, world trade will shrink sharply next year, but will continue to grow.
The WTO stressed the uncertainty surrounding the forecast due to "monetary policy changes in developed economies and the unpredictable nature of the Russia-Ukraine war."
The WTO has warned that if the situation worsens, trade growth next year could be as low as -2.8 percent, but stressed that if things change in a positive direction, it could rise to 4.6 percent. According to the AFP news agency.
