Indonesia has been exempting palm oil exports from mid-July to help reduce rising palm oil stocks following a three-week export ban in late April. Up to stabilize domestic cooking oil prices.
The current tax exemption policy will end after October 31.
Indonesia levies export taxes in addition to a separate export tax to raise money for subsidies for biodiesel and palm oil replanting programs for smallholder farmers.
However, Airlangga said that as palm oil prices have fallen and biodiesel produced from palm oil is cheaper than Indonesian fossil fuels, there is now a need for less funding for biodiesel programs.
Palm oil exporters are required to sell some of their products in the country before being allowed to export. The company is currently allowed to export nine times the volume they sell locally. According to Reuters.
