MOSCOW (Reuters) - On September 30, after a referendum on Ukraine, which the West called "illegal," the Russian government declared four regions of Ukraine part of its territory. Russia officially. The announcement comes amid a "partial Russian mobilization" that has left some Russians dissatisfied and fearful of fleeing the country to avoid being sent to war in Ukraine.
With the declaration of annexation of Ukraine and the deployment of troops, it shows that the Ukrainian war has entered a new phase that seems "there is no turning back". Putin has made it clear that he will do everything in his power, including a "nuclear deadline", to win the war in Ukraine.
By putting an end to Putin's use of nuclear weapons and mobilization, it is also putting pressure on both the Russian people and the Russian leadership, whether they must follow Putin to the end of the world or stand by. Do you see Russia losing or facing retaliation from the West? Not only that, ambitions for Ukraine have also put Putin on a vulnerable position.
At this stage, Putin may still believe that Russia has the potential to win. In his opinion, additional troop deployments could help the Russian army oust the Ukrainian army from the occupied territories and force the West to gradually liberate Ukraine, leaving Kiev to surrender. Paving the way for Russia to take control of new territories cut off from Ukraine.
"I do not have exact figures, but it is definitely far from what has been claimed," the spokesman said.
Tens of thousands of Russians, mostly military men, have fled the country in order to avoid being called up for military service in Ukraine. Kazakhstan, Georgia and Mongolia, all of which share borders with Russia, reported an increase in the number of border crossings following Putin's announcement.
