SEOUL (Reuters) - Foreign investors continued to sell Asian stocks, excluding China, in September on rising US interest rates, a stronger dollar and a slowdown in the region's growth outlook. According to Reuters.
Reuters reported citing data from South Korea, India, Taiwan, the Philippines, Vietnam, Indonesia and Thailand that foreign investors sold $ 8.83 billion worth of shares last month, their first monthly sale. They have been since June.
To date, regional stocks have faced a total capital outflow of $ 69.7 billion in the first quarter of this year, up sharply from the $ 47.63 billion outflow experienced in the first quarter. 2008 When the global financial crisis occurred.
