Mekong River Commission Launches Mekong River Monitoring Technology Competition
(Phnom Penh): The Mekong River Commission (MRC) has announced the launch of a special competition program that encourages local university students to develop sustainable and effective technologies to monitor Mekong River water levels, rainfall, soil moisture and water quality.
The launch of the MRC River Tracking Technology Competition aims to provide opportunities for highly innovative students in Cambodia, Laos, Thailand and Vietnam from one of 15 pre-selected universities for the pool area. The entire Lower Mekong River to help save the lives of millions of families, fishermen and farmers.
The MRC maintains more than 250 monitoring stations that monitor hydrology, rainfall, water quality, ecological health, fisheries and drought, affecting the largest rivers in Southeast Asia. However, most of these tracking technologies rely heavily on foreign equipment, which is often expensive and sometimes obsolete.
While the MRC values its international partners, Dr. Anulak Kithi Khuon, Executive Director of the MRC Secretariat, said that local solutions should be low cost, build the capacity of local professionals and provide other benefits, such as training. That the people along the Mekong River can be able to solve the Mekong River problems through technological innovation.
Winning entries will be judged on accuracy, durability, cost effectiveness and innovation.
The competition is open to undergraduate or postgraduate students studying at one of the 15 partner universities located in the four MRC member countries of Cambodia, Laos, Thailand and Vietnam.
At the end of March, each candidate will present a prototype to an international jury in Vientiane, which will select the four winners, one for each division / discipline.
In addition to the $ 5,000 prize money, the winners will be invited to Vientiane to present their projects to local heads of government and other dignitaries in April 2023 at the MRC Summit. Fourth and an international seminar before the MRC Summit.
After that, the winner will work with MRC to implement its successful technology for the Mekong River.
